      "raw": "# AI Intelligence Briefing - August 31, 2026\n*Curated from knowledge graph (796 nodes, 837 edges) · All articles published within the last 7 days*\n*(Backfilled on 2026-09-01)*\n\n• **Nutanix Cloud Platform Updates Expand Capabilities for Production Agentic AI** — Nutanix announced Enterprise AI 2.8 (generally available now) and Kubernetes Platform 2.19 (\"available soon\"), positioning its \"dual-native\" architecture so agentic AI workloads can run alongside existing virtualized and containerized applications under common management and governance. The headline feature for agent builders is generally available MCP server management in the Nutanix Agent Gateway — a centralized connection point between AI agents and MCP servers with tool permissions assignable to specific users or API keys, plus rolling updates for locally deployed servers. NAI 2.8 also extends private inference: fine-tuning for models under 8B parameters, NVIDIA NIM microservices deployable in air-gapped environments, and tech previews of multi-node/multi-GPU inference for 100B+ parameter models and KV cache offloading from GPU to host memory.\n  🔗 Graph: nutanix, model-context-protocol, kubernetes, agentic-ai, llm-gateway\n  📅 Published: 2026-08-31\n  📰 https://campustechnology.com/articles/2026/08/31/nutanix-cloud-platform-updates-expand-capabilities-for-production-agentic-ai.aspx\n  📌 Key takeaways:\n    • MCP server governance — per-user and per-API-key tool permissions — is moving from concept to shipping enterprise infrastructure, giving security teams an actual control plane for agent tool access.\n    • The \"dual-native\" pitch (run agents next to existing VMs and containers, no rebuild required) lowers the barrier for hybrid estates that can't lift-and-shift to get agentic AI.\n    • Air-gapped NIM deployment plus sub-8B fine-tuning targets the regulated-workload objection directly; treat the 100B+ multi-GPU inference as future, not current, capability.\n\n• **CSA's Top Cloud Threats: Identity, AI** — The Cloud Security Alliance's 2026 Top Threats report — a survey of 507 security professionals ranking 23 cloud security issues — ranks inadequate identity and access management first, displacing misconfiguration (down to No. 5), with insecure third-party resources rising to No. 3 and two AI-related categories entering the Top 11 for the first time. The 2026 ranking marks a shift from infrastructure-centric concerns toward identity, AI, software supply chains, and interconnected ecosystems: DoS, shared technology vulnerabilities, and CSP data loss all fell out of the Top 11. CSA positions the report for security program planning, risk prioritization, and governance investment decisions across cloud and AI environments.\n  🔗 Graph: ai-security, ai-governance, ai-compliance-governance, enterprise-monitoring\n  📅 Published: 2026-08-31\n  📰 https://campustechnology.com/articles/2026/08/31/csas-top-cloud-threats-identity-ai.aspx\n  📌 Key takeaways:\n    • Identity-first security is now the consensus top cloud risk — and AI agents holding their own credentials will expand that attack surface faster than most IAM programs are prepared for.\n    • Two AI threat categories entering the Top 11 in one survey cycle confirms AI risk has moved from \"emerging\" to standing governance line item.\n    • The tight score spread (7.95 to 7.45 across the Top 11) argues for portfolio-based risk management rather than chasing a single top threat.\n\n• **Why Trust Matters as Higher Ed Adopts AI Tools** — EdTech Magazine examines trust as the gating factor for AI adoption on campus, opening from the displacement anxiety — \"Will AI eventually be able to replace humans? Will automation render our skills obsolete?\" — that remains a major barrier to adoption across industries, and institutions' responses to it. (Full text paywalled at source; verified headline, URL, and publication date from the RSS feed.)\n  🔗 Graph: higher-ed-ai, ai-adoption, ai-governance\n  📅 Published: 2026-08-31\n  📰 https://edtechmagazine.com/higher/article/2026/08/why-trust-matters-higher-ed-adopts-ai-tools\n  📌 Key takeaways:\n    • Displacement fear is an adoption barrier that no amount of tooling fixes — it has to be addressed in governance and communication.\n    • Trust is the frame to use when introducing AI tools to faculty and staff, not capability claims.\n"
